<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel de Champlain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1567–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel de Champlain was a French explorer, navigator and colonial administrator who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> in 1608. He explored much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> region and built alliances with Indigenous peoples to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> credit him with establishing the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and the first lasting French colonial presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain.jpg" alt="Samuel de Champlain" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel de Champlain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samchamprifle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first colonial empire took root only in 1608, when <b>Samuel de Champlain</b> founded Quebec as the capital of New France and the centre of the fur trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The hostility of New France began when <b>Samuel de Champlain</b> and his Huron and Algonquin allies killed several Mohawk leaders near Lake Champlain in 1609.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a first settlement at Port-Royal in Acadia in 1605, <b>Samuel de Champlain</b> founded Quebec in 1608 as a fur-trading post, allied himself with the Montagnais, Algonquin and Huron, and explored as far as Lake Huron.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The colony on the St Lawrence had its capital at Quebec, founded by <b>Samuel de Champlain</b> in 1608.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samuel de Champlain</b> established a fortified trading post at Quebec on the St Lawrence.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1604 Pierre Dugua de Mons and <b>Samuel de Champlain</b> wintered on Saint Croix Island, and in 1605 moved across the Bay of Fundy to found Port-Royal.</blockquote><a href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hiers-Brouage, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Quebec City, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.85,-1.08&amp;year=1611" aria-label="Hiers-Brouage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4525 548 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel de Champlain"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 818h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 818h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.85,-1.08&amp;year=1611">What was here in 1611? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Of all the most useful and excellent arts, that of navigation has always seemed to me to occupy the first place.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to the Queen Regent, Voyages (1613) &#183; tr. Charles Pomeroy Otis, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6749/pg6749-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;One might judge, if the territory four leagues in extent lying between Panama and this river were cut through, he could pass from the south sea to that on the other side, and thus shorten the route by more than fifteen hundred leagues.&#8221;</p><footer>Brief Discours, on the Isthmus of Panama (c. 1601) &#183; tr. in the Prince Society edition of his Voyages, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6653/pg6653-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…this is the art which in my early years won my love, and has induced me to expose myself almost all my life to the impetuous waves of the ocean.&#8221;</p><footer>Voyages (1613), address to the Queen Regent, on navigation &#183; tr. Charles Pomeroy Otis, 1880 &#183; quoted in Edmund F. Slafter's memoir of Champlain &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6653/pg6653-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
